    "en": {
      "shared_perspectives": "German media across the spectrum agree that demographic ageing is eroding the fiscal balance of the statutory pension system and that without structural adjustments the scheme will become unsustainable for future generations. They also converge on the observation that a sizable share of the working population doubts they will be able to reach the current retirement age, linking this scepticism to rising old-age poverty and persistent pension gaps, especially in the Länder with lower average earnings. Finally, there is a shared recognition that any reform must reconcile the protection of low-wage and mini-job earners with the need for a stronger capital-based component, even though the preferred mix of public versus private provision is contested.",
      "controversial_points": "The debate pits market-oriented reforms against the welfare state's guarantee of universal pensions. Liberals stress intergenerational equity through private capital accounts, while leftists warn of widening poverty gaps. Conservatives defend the retirement age as a fiscal necessity, opposing calls for earlier exits and higher benefits. Populist and far-right outlets frame pension cuts as a democratic deficit, linking them to elite corruption."
